WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.680
Few child actors have had a life story as&nbsp;
redemptive as iCarly's Jennette McCurdy. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.200
her TV days are over, her life was forever changed&nbsp;
by the show — for better or sometimes worse.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.720
Becoming a child star was never an aspiration&nbsp;
for Jennette McCurdy, but rather for her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.480
Debra. McCurdy alleged that it was her mom&nbsp;
who had wanted to become an actor. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.240
those dreams didn't happen, Debra relentlessly&nbsp;
pursued that dream with her daughter. McCurdy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.680
childhood home life was also troubled. She&nbsp;
was homeschooled in a religious household,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.360
her parents constantly fought and&nbsp;
her mother was a compulsive hoarder.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.040
McCurdy said that most of the obsessive behavior&nbsp;
was attributed to her mother's health at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.840
In Jennette's infancy, her mother was diagnosed&nbsp;
with breast cancer. The actor said that her mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.360
began living vicariously through her and sent&nbsp;
McCurdy into the world of child acting. Debra also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.400
routinely examined her daughter's body for signs&nbsp;
of cancer, among other troubling behaviors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.520
deeply traumatized McCurdy. It became difficult&nbsp;
for the Nickelodeon star to challenge her mother's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.080
authority, as she would often remind McCurdy how&nbsp;
lucky she was for her early success on iCarly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.800
In her memoir, I’m Glad My Mom Died, McCurdy&nbsp;
writes that Debra would constantly tell her,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.520
"Everyone wants what you have."

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.640
The pressure on Jennette McCurdy only got worse&nbsp;
the more successful iCarly became. Desperate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.920
to keep up appearances on television, McCurdy's&nbsp;
mother taught her to develop an eating disorder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.440
when she was only eleven years old. As&nbsp;
McCurdy later recounted in a Red Table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:31.360
Talk with Jada Pinkett-Smith, her mom was the&nbsp;
one who encouraged her to restrict calories.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.320
"We were just in the disease, in the&nbsp;
sickness. But there was a connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.160
that the sickness created that I, of&nbsp;
course, couldn't see at the time."

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:43.560
McCurdy's struggles with anorexia and&nbsp;
bulimia followed her into iCarly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.680
beyond. She was limiting herself to under 1,000&nbsp;
calories a day in an attempt to make her mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.160
proud. Even after the death of McCurdy's&nbsp;
mother, the eating disorder grew worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.320
with the actor at one point collapsing&nbsp;
in co-star Miranda Cosgrove's bathroom.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.120
Many child stars from Disney Channel&nbsp;
and Nickelodeon have attempted music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.880
careers after the success of stars Selena&nbsp;
Gomez and Miley Cyrus. In her 2022 memoir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.200
I'm Glad My Mom Died, McCurdy revealed that when&nbsp;
iCarly was shut down due to the 2007 WGA Strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.320
her mother pushed her into pursuing country music.&nbsp;
In 2009, she signed a record deal with Capitol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.080
Records, turning down an offer from Taylor&nbsp;
Swift's then-home label Big Machine Records.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.360
After releasing several singles and EPs, McCurdy&nbsp;
released her debut self-titled album in 2012. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.520
McCurdy's music career didn't last long,&nbsp;
as the Nickelodeon star ended up leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.360
Capitol Records because of a commitment to&nbsp;
her new iCarly spin-off show, Sam &amp; Cat.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.480
McCurdy expressed regret over her country&nbsp;
music days in her memoir, though the brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:49.520
stint offered one huge shift in her life. Debra's&nbsp;
cancer returned and McCurdy was able to go on tour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.320
without her. It was the first time the two were&nbsp;
separated since McCurdy was a young child. But her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.120
solo excursion ended up creating more guilt&nbsp;
for McCurdy. She told The Guardian in 2022,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.200
"God did it feel s---ty to feel relief&nbsp;
that I'm going to be away from my mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.520
for the first time, when my mom also was&nbsp;
just diagnosed with cancer and was dying."

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.240
Nickelodeon's iCarly ended its run in 2012,&nbsp;
though Jennette McCurdy didn't stray far from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.920
the network in its immediate aftermath.&nbsp;
In 2013, the actor starred in a spin-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.800
series with Ariana Grande, Sam &amp; Cat. When&nbsp;
McCurdy initially signed onto the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.960
she was under the impression that it&nbsp;
was meant to be a solo series for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.480
character, Sam Puckett. Nickelodeon instead&nbsp;
re-developed it to include Grande's character.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.160
While filming Sam &amp; Cat, things started to spin&nbsp;
out of Jennette McCurdy's favor at the network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:41.480
She revealed in her memoir that Grande was&nbsp;
consistently given preferential treatment on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:47.120
program, missing entire weeks to pursue her music&nbsp;
career. McCurdy was forced to turn down several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:53.120
film offers due to her contractual obligations&nbsp;
to Sam &amp; Cat, a standard Grande was not held to.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
McCurdy has since softened towards Grande&nbsp;
in recent years. She told Vogue in 2022,

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.720
"I'm able to look back on it and just&nbsp;
see it as the comedy gold that it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.520
to be on set at 21, where you're just the most&nbsp;
susceptible to jealousy and comparison that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:11.180
you can possibly be, and I'm there across&nbsp;
from a burgeoning pop star of the day."

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.300
"Oh! aahh, ah Oh.”

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.101
“Is it that bad?”

00:04:15.101 --> 00:04:16.741
“Nah, you barely notice it."

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.760
A good portion of Jennette McCurdy's memoir,&nbsp;
I'm Glad My Mom Died, recounts her experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.360
with The Creator, a pseudonym for iCarly and&nbsp;
Sam &amp; Cat creator Dan Schneider. Schneider's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.320
behavior was common knowledge by the time&nbsp;
of the book's release; Nickelodeon severed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.800
ties with him in 2018, and he has since been&nbsp;
accused of verbal abuse and sexual misconduct.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.720
McCurdy's memoir only added fuel to the fire.&nbsp;
She claimed that he intimidated her into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.880
underage drinking, among other inappropriate&nbsp;
actions he committed towards her. She wrote,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.040
"The Creator is doing the thing that I've&nbsp;
heard from my co-stars he does with every new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.280
star of a show that he's making — he takes you&nbsp;
under his wing. You're his favorite. For now."

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.640
McCurdy began to rely heavily&nbsp;
on alcohol due to the stress and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.760
abuse she was facing. She opened&nbsp;
up about her relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:07.800
alcohol for the first time in a 2020&nbsp;
episode of her podcast, Empty Inside.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.600
As if the treatment Jennette McCurdy&nbsp;
faced from Nickelodeon on iCarly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.560
Sam &amp; Cat wasn't enough, the actor was also&nbsp;
limited in her opportunities outside of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.360
network. Aside from having to turn down movie&nbsp;
offers due to Nickelodeon-related commitments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.800
McCurdy instead began making appearances&nbsp;
in similar projects on Nickelodeon.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.560
Her guest spots on other popular Nickelodeon shows&nbsp;
included True Jackson, VP, and Victorious. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.520
her frequent appearances got her nominations at&nbsp;
the Teen Choice Awards and Kids' Choice Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:41.840
there was little career benefit the older she got.&nbsp;
It wasn't until after iCarly and Sam &amp; Cat were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.160
over that McCurdy branched out to more serious&nbsp;
roles. She appeared in the 2016 thriller Pet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.800
and most notably on the Netflix drama Between,&nbsp;
in which she starred in for two seasons.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.960
In 2018, she made her directorial&nbsp;
debut with a short film, Kenny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.520
which premiered on YouTube's Short&nbsp;
of the Week. McCurdy was especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.480
proud of the project, having assembled&nbsp;
an all-female crew to help her make it.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.160
McCurdy didn't have the benefit of&nbsp;
leaving Nickelodeon on good terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:11.840
She stated in her memoir that the network&nbsp;
offered her $300,000 to stay quiet about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.040
her experiences with Dan Schnider. Though&nbsp;
she ultimately turned the hush money down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:20.200
she briefly regretted the decision. These&nbsp;
days she's proud that she chose the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.200
of morality rather than caving to&nbsp;
the network that exploited her.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.640
The death of Jennette McCurdy's mother Debra was&nbsp;
a pivotal moment in the young actor's life. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.720
mother died in 2013, just three years after&nbsp;
receiving word that her cancer had returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:38.920
when McCurdy was only 21 years old. Even without&nbsp;
the monumental figure in her life controlling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:43.680
her every decision, it took years before&nbsp;
McCurdy could reconcile with the damage done.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.360
McCurdy believes the kind of healing she&nbsp;
was able to do wouldn't be possible with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.200
her mother still alive, stating that therapy&nbsp;
wasn't an option at the time. Even when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.080
finally started to seek help, it was a&nbsp;
long road to figure out how she really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.960
felt. She recounted on The View that it was&nbsp;
tough going to the first therapist she saw.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.000
"She said, 'You know, what you're&nbsp;
describing is abuse,' and I left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.520
I quit therapy. I couldn't tolerate the&nbsp;
idea, the notion that my mom was abusive."

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.240
McCurdy takes great pride in her ongoing mental&nbsp;
health journey, which she credits to unpacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.920
years of emotional and psychological&nbsp;
abuse at the hands of her mother.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:24.840
In 2014, Sam &amp; Cat was canceled after one season,&nbsp;
ending Jennette McCurdy's seven-year tenure on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.160
Nickelodeon. A lifetime's worth of trauma&nbsp;
had occurred to the actor during that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.960
but within a year she would make the first steps&nbsp;
towards shaping the rest of her career. McCurdy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.960
began with creating, writing, and starring&nbsp;
in her own web series, What's Next For Sarah?

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.760
The autobiographical four-episode&nbsp;
show finds McCurdy playing Sarah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:48.120
whose hit tween TV show is canceled, throwing her&nbsp;
life in disarray as she tries to figure out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.160
to do next. The cast of What's Next For Sarah?&nbsp;
starred many Nickelodeon friends of McCurdy's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.720
including Mary Scheer, Shayne&nbsp;
Topp, and Danielle Morrow.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.800
The show was McCurdy's first opportunity to&nbsp;
grapple with what her career in entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.880
has meant on her own terms. She's continued to&nbsp;
write and create art about what's happened to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:11.840
while simultaneously pursuing a career that has&nbsp;
kept her far away from her Nickelodeon past.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.720
There are one too many stories in&nbsp;
Hollywood of child actors being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.640
taken advantage of by the adults in&nbsp;
their lives. McCurdy was no different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.720
given the extreme control exerted on&nbsp;
her life and career by her mother. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.440
McCurdy's acting success was a fulfillment&nbsp;
of her mom's lifelong dream to be a star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.320
there was also a practical purpose for it.&nbsp;
Through her acting gigs, Jennette became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.480
the breadwinner of her family, supporting her&nbsp;
parents, grandparents and three older brothers.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.280
But McCurdy didn't receive any of the money she&nbsp;
earned working as an actor. Her Coogan Account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.360
which is meant to shave off 15% of the salary&nbsp;
a child actor makes into a savings account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.800
was never properly filed. As a result,&nbsp;
McCurdy is unaware of what happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.320
all of the money or where it went, but it's&nbsp;
believed to have been stolen by her mom.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.600
McCurdy's hard work paid off in other regards&nbsp;
later in life. In 2022, she released her New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.720
Times Best Selling memoir all about the abuse she&nbsp;
suffered from her mother. McCurdy confirmed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.120
her older brothers were in favor of her decision&nbsp;
to speak out. She told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.760
"My brothers have been so supportive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.320
so understanding. They get&nbsp;
the title, to put it simply."

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:20.600
In 2020, McCurdy debuted her first one-woman&nbsp;
show, I'm Glad My Mom Died, which was her most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.000
vulnerable work to date. She was initially&nbsp;
hesitant about performing the show herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:29.560
though she eventually realized that only she&nbsp;
could tell her own story. The stage show ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.880
only for a few months at the start of 2020 before&nbsp;
the COVID-19 pandemic shut down live theater.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.160
During the pandemic, McCurdy continued the&nbsp;
conversations about her past on her podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:45.280
series, Empty Inside. With guests like Anna Faris,&nbsp;
Mike Birbiglia, and Daniel Sloss, she opened up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.200
about her mom, her relationship with alcohol,&nbsp;
and her career. After reviving the one-woman show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:56.680
in 2021, she turned her story into a memoir in&nbsp;
2022. I'm Glad My Mom Died sparked lots of media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:01.600
attention as she came forward about the abuse she&nbsp;
suffered from both her mother and Nickelodeon.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.040
"I think that anything worth saying is&nbsp;
probably going to be divisive. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.080
if it's not divisive, it's probably, there's&nbsp;
probably no new conversation to be had there."

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.360
The book almost immediately sold out on&nbsp;
online retailers and remained at number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.120
one on The New York Times bestseller&nbsp;
list for eight weeks. McCurdy has since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.440
signed a new deal to write books for Penguin&nbsp;
Random House. She told The New York Times:

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:27.560
"Felt like finally I'm saying my words and&nbsp;
saying things I want to be saying. I'm myself."

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:33.320
McCurdy's life has been quite the rollercoaster&nbsp;
ever since she was first cast on iCarly in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:38.520
Though she's been through a lot of unimaginable&nbsp;
tragedy and harrowing trauma, McCurdy has come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:44.760
on the other side stronger and braver. In 2021,&nbsp;
Paramount+ made the decision to reboot iCarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.160
bringing back Miranda Cosgrove, Nathan&nbsp;
Kress, and Jerry Trainor to re-inhabit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:53.880
the characters they had last played in&nbsp;
2012. But McCurdy said no thank you.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.480
She described in her memoir that Cosgrove&nbsp;
contacted her personally to invite her to reprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:03.200
the role of Sam Puckett. Despite Cosgrove's&nbsp;
encouragement, McCurdy refused to return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.520
not wanting to retread the experience that&nbsp;
caused her so much pain throughout her life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.640
Nathan Kress later addressed McCurdy's absence&nbsp;
on his own podcast, RadioActive Dads, saying:

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.680
"I think it's just a personal decision and&nbsp;
where she's at and what she wants to do."

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:19.120
McCurdy has stated that she&nbsp;
doesn't miss the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:23.240
of being on iCarly one bit. She knows&nbsp;
she's plenty busy with her book deals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:27.800
her new podcast Hard Feelings, and&nbsp;
her plans for writing and directing.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.920
